$$
\bar{x}_{t + 1}= \bar{y}_{t}- \eta \bar{G}_{t},\tag{19}
$$

$$
\bar{y}_{t+1}=\bar{x}_{t+1}+\frac{1-\alpha}{1+\alpha}(\bar{x}_{t+1}-\bar{x}_{t}),\tag{20}
$$

$$
\bar{s}_{t + 1}= \bar{s}_{t}+ \bar{g}_{t + 1}- \bar{g}_{t}= \bar{g}_{t + 1}.\tag{21}
$$

The algorithm below replaces the ${\cal O}(\log(n/\delta))$ term of the naive bound by ${\cal O}(\log(1/\delta))$. Its idea is to discard the worse half of the arms in every iteration. The best arm need not come out empirically "the best"; all that is required is that some ε-optimal arm lies above the median.

Algorithm 4 (Median Elimination).
Given: $\varepsilon > 0$ and $\delta > 0$. Returns: one arm.
1. Initialise $S_{1}= A$, $\varepsilon_{1}= \varepsilon/ 4$, $\delta_{1}= \delta / 2$, $\ell = 1$.
2. Loop body:
   (a) draw $1/(\varepsilon_{\ell}/2)^{2}\log(3/\delta_{\ell})$ samples from each arm $a \in S_{\ell}$, writing $\hat{p}_{a}^{\ell}$ for its empirical value;
   (b) let $m_{\ell}$ be the median of the values $\hat{p}_{a}^{\ell}$;
   (c) set $S_{\ell + 1}= S_{\ell}\backslash \{a : \hat{p}_{a}^{\ell}< m_{\ell}\}$;
   (d) update $\varepsilon_{\ell + 1}= \frac{3}{4}\varepsilon_{\ell}$ and $\delta_{\ell + 1}= \delta_{\ell}/ 2$, then $\ell = \ell + 1$.
3. Repeat step 2 until $| S_{\ell}| = 1$.

